From: INMYS <support@inmys.ru>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/bridge: sn65dsi83: clear the latched error status before
 resetting the pipe

REG_IRQ_STAT (CSR 0xe5) is RW1C: the bits stay set until a one is written
back.  sn65dsi83_handle_errors() reads it and, on any bit other than
CHA_PLL_UNLOCK, schedules a pipe reset - but it never clears the bit.

The SN65DSI84 latches a CHA_SYNCH_ERR while the DSI link starts up, and
resetting the pipe latches another one.  So a single start-up glitch turns
into an endless reset loop: the pipe is reset roughly once a second and
never gets a chance to run.  On the ELVEES MCom-03R with the bridge in
dual-link mode this showed up as

    sn65dsi83 9-002d: reset the pipe

repeating forever, while the LVDS clocks and all eight data lanes were
measuring correctly on the far end.  Clearing CSR 0xe5 from user space
stopped the loop and the status then stayed at zero for as long as it was
watched, which is what identifies the bit as a start-up transient rather
than a live fault.

Clear what was latched and only reset if it comes back.  That is what the
datasheet's own init sequence prescribes: write 0xff to CSR 0xe5, wait
1 ms, and re-initialise if CSR 0xe5 is still not zero.

Signed-off-by: INMYS <support@inmys.ru>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/ti-sn65dsi83.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/bridge/ti-sn65dsi83.c
@@ -436,6 +436,27 @@
 	 * problems. In lack of a reliable way to discriminate such cases
 	 * from user-visible PLL_UNLOCK cases, ignore that bit entirely.
 	 */
+	if (!ret && irq_stat & ~REG_IRQ_STAT_CHA_PLL_UNLOCK) {
+		/*
+		 * REG_IRQ_STAT is RW1C, so the bits stay set until a one is
+		 * written back.  The bridge latches a CHA_SYNCH_ERR while the
+		 * DSI link starts up, and resetting the pipe latches another
+		 * one, so acting on the first read turns a single start-up
+		 * glitch into an endless reset loop - the pipe is reset every
+		 * second and never gets a chance to run.  Seen on the ELVEES
+		 * MCom-03R with the SN65DSI84 in dual-link mode.
+		 *
+		 * Clear what was latched and only reset if it comes back,
+		 * which is what the datasheet's init sequence does: write
+		 * 0xff to CSR 0xe5, then re-initialise if it is still not
+		 * zero.
+		 */
+		regmap_write(ctx->regmap, REG_IRQ_STAT, irq_stat);
+		usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+
+		ret = regmap_read(ctx->regmap, REG_IRQ_STAT, &irq_stat);
+	}
+
 	if (ret || irq_stat & ~REG_IRQ_STAT_CHA_PLL_UNLOCK) {
 		/*
 		 * IRQ acknowledged is not always possible (the bridge can be in
